{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And Hamor spoke, etc.</b> Its saying <b>saying</b> — perhaps it intends to say that he is prepared to carry out the written law: the law of one who violates a virgin is to pay the penalty of fine, shame, and blemish. He is also prepared to take her as a wife if her father and brothers so wish. And that is what he says: <b>Give</b>, etc.:" "<b>Or</b> it may mean that he intended to exempt himself from the fine, as we learned (Ketubot 41a): one who says 'I violated, I seduced, so-and-so's daughter' — does not pay the fine on his own admission. If so, this is why it says <b>saying: Shechem my son</b>, etc. — meaning that Shechem admits on his own and says that he lay with Dinah, and thereby he is exempt from the fixed liability that is the fine, whether in the case of a seducer or of a violator. And one should not explain that Hamor is testifying on behalf of Shechem, for under the laws of the children of Noah the son is held liable on the testimony of his father (Rambam, Laws of Kings 9:14)."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:56.447341882Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}